				section.document-section#2-scope-assumptions-and-key-corrections
					h2
						span.icon.section-heading__icon.icon--scope(aria-hidden="true")
						span 2. Scope, assumptions and key corrections
					
					aside.callout
						strong Legal status:
						|  This is a task-specific working assessment, not a substitute for the festival’s Event Safety Plan, fire-risk assessment, licence conditions, construction-phase arrangements or a competent person’s site inspection. It can support compliance only after the actual employers, self-employed persons, suppliers and persons in control have confirmed their duties, completed the Priority A actions, consulted affected workers and approved the controlled issue. No signature transfers a legal duty from the person or organisation that holds it.
					
					ul(role="list")
						li PhotoSYNTH uses a camera for real-time facial/movement control and can present moving visual content. All camera processing takes place locally and in real time on the device; no video footage is stored or transmitted. The deployed version and visual-effect settings must be verified before opening and after any software, configuration or equipment change.
						li The assessment records significant findings for the PhotoSYNTH activity and people affected by it. Each employer and self-employed dutyholder remains responsible for a suitable and sufficient assessment of risks arising from its own undertaking, including risks to workers, contractors, volunteers and members of the public.
						li Controls follow the hierarchy of prevention: avoid the hazard where reasonably practicable, then use engineering and collective controls, safe systems, information/training and supervision; personal protective equipment is a last line of protection and must be provided without charge where legally required.
						li The Equality Act duty to make reasonable adjustments for disabled service users is anticipatory and continuing. Access must be designed in before opening, reviewed during operation and provided without charging the disabled person for the adjustment.
						li If anyone under 18 works on the activity, or a worker notifies their employer in writing that they are pregnant, breastfeeding or have given birth within the previous six months, the relevant employer must complete and act on the additional or individual assessment required for that worker before assigning duties.
			
